    "topic_code": "2.6",
    "topic_title": "Derivative Rules: Constant, Sum, Difference, and Constant Multiple",
    "stem": "Let g and h be differentiable functions with g′(2) = 5 and h′(2) = −2. Define f(x) = 4g(x) − 3h(x) + 7. What is f′(2)?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "33",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Constant-retention error: adding 7 to the derivative gives 33, but the derivative of a constant is 0. Fix: Replace each constant term with 0 before evaluating the derivative."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "14",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Sign error: subtracting 3h′(2) means subtracting −6, not subtracting 6. The subtraction sign in f remains when differentiating. Fix: Substitute negative derivative values in parentheses before simplifying signs."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "26",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Differentiate term by term, preserving each coefficient and subtraction sign; the constant 7 has derivative 0. Thus f′(2) = 4g′(2) − 3h′(2) = 4(5) − 3(−2) = 26."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "7",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Constant-multiple error: 5 − (−2) = 7 omits the coefficients 4 and 3. These coefficients must multiply the corresponding derivatives. Fix: Copy each coefficient beside its function’s derivative before substituting values."
     }
    ]
   },
